{
  "$comment": "WHERE EACH CONVERTED FIXTURE FAMILY'S SEED LIVES, WHAT IT ANSWERS, AND WHICH OF ITS FIELDS ARE JOINED FROM OTHER SEEDS. No family is re-derived from the engine any more, so nothing here projects: a family declares the contract schema its seed `answers` (read by check-mock-seeds.py's schema arm), the seed `file` it is written to, and, where its entries carry fields another family holds, the `join` that re-derives them (both read by scripts/build-mock-seeds.py for the correspondence guard).",
  "$operations": {
    "answers": "the contract schema this family's seed must validate against. A tiny expression: `Name` is `#/components/schemas/Name`, a lowercase name is a primitive type, `X[]` is a list of X, and `{X}` is a map whose values are X. It is declared PER FAMILY rather than read off the operation, because most operations compose several families into one envelope — the acquisition queue is five buckets and the media sheet is seven families — so validating a family against its operation's whole response schema would report a mismatch for every one of them.",
    "file": "the seed file this family is written to, WITHOUT its extension. Declared rather than derived from the family's own name, because the family names are the ENGINE's — French in one case, abbreviated in several — while these files are new and are named in English, written out in full, on the day they are written. The `_REAL` suffix the engine uses for the calm scenario becomes the bare name here, and the dense one carries `-loaded`: the engine's suffix says which is the exception, and it is the other way round.",
    "join": "fields ADDED after the projection, from other families, and never from the family's own literal. `entries` names where the entries are — `$item` each element of a top-level list, `$value` each value of a top-level map, `/key[]` each element of the list under that key, `[]/key[]` each element of the list under that key in each element of a top-level list, `[]/key` the object under that key in each element that carries one — and `fields` maps each added field to its source: `ids` the provider identity of the media sheet the entry's title resolves to (null when none), `poster` the poster under the entry's title, then under its title without the year (null when absent), `exactPoster` the poster under the entry's own title only (null when absent), `key` the entry's own top-level key. The lossless comparison reads the projection before the join."
  },
